ed to make a focused argumentative contribution, state our current recommendation, refer to discussion, use explicit facts, explain implication.\n\nWe have our own notes: Candidate A: correct decisions quickly, difficulty communicating ideas, egocentric, not willing to further education, puts concern for others above everything, excellent attention skills. Candidate B: very conscientious, handles stress very well, good at assessing weather conditions, excellent computer skills, regarded as pretentious, sometimes adopts wrong tone when communicating. Candidate D: responds to unexpected events adequately, can concentrate very well, solves problems extremely well, takes responsibility seriously, considered moody, regarded as a loner.\n\nWe need to consider criteria: reliability, stress resilience, technical competence, calmness under pressure, cooperation, responsibility, etc.\n\nWe need to decide our vote. We need to consider the evidence. Candidate C strong technical and leadership but concerns about feedback and unorganized, no crew cooperation evidence. Candidate B strong stress resilience, conscientiousness (reliability), good weather assessment (technical), but communication tone issues and pretentiousness (crew cooperation). Candidate A quick decision, excellent attention, puts concern for others above everything (cooperation?), but difficulty communicating ideas, egocentric, not willing to further education (responsibility/maturity). Candidate D strong problem solving, concentration, responsibility, but moody, loner (cooperation), considered moody (reliability?), arrogance? Not in notes but moody and loner.\n\nWe need to weigh. Which candidate best overall? Possibly Candidate B: stress handling, conscientiousness, technical weather assessment, but communication tone issues. Candidate A has communication difficulty and egocentric, which is serious for crew. Candidate D moody and loner, also problematic. Candidate C lacks crew cooperation evidence and feedback orientation issues.\n\nThus maybe Candidate B is the strongest alternative. We need to argue for B or maybe still C? We need to decide.\n\nGiven Agent 2's caution, we could either maintain C or shift to B. Let's evaluate: C's lack of crew cooperation evidence is a gap; feedback orientation is a concern. B has known stress resilience and conscientiousness, but also communication tone issues (pretentious, wrong tone). Which is more critical? Crew cooperation and communication are essential. B's communication issues are noted: sometimes adopts wrong tone, pretentious. That could affect crew harmony. C's lack of evidence could be unknown; but we have explicit note that C sometimes not good at taking criticism, which is a feedback orientation issue. That is also important.\n\nWe might argue that B's strengths in stress resilience and technical competence outweigh his communication tone issues, which could be mitigated with training, whereas C's lack of crew cooperation evidence is a bigger unknown risk. So we could shift vote to B.\n\nThus our public message: I agree with Agent 2 that we need to compare B and A, but I think B is the best overall given his stress handling, conscientiousness, technical weather assessment, and responsibility, despite communication tone concerns. I will vote Candidate B.\n\nWe need to reference explicit facts: B very conscientious (reliability), handles stress very well (stress resilience), good at assessing weather (technical competence), but sometimes adopts wrong tone (communication). Compare to C's lack of crew cooperation evidence and feedback orientation issues.\n\nThus produce public message and metadata JSON with vote Candidate B."}
